200. Given an array, check whether any number is the product of all the remaining numbers.

   Example:
   Input:  {2, 3, 6}
   Output: true

   Explanation:
   2 * 3 = 6, so 6 is the product of the remaining elements.

   Java code:

   public static boolean checkProduct(int[] arr) {

       if (arr == null || arr.length < 2) {
           return false;
       }

       long product = 1;

       for (int num : arr) {
           product *= num;
       }

       for (int num : arr) {
           if (num != 0 && product % num == 0
                   && product / num == num) {
               return true;
           }

           if (num == 0) {
               long otherProduct = 1;
               int zeroCount = 0;

               for (int value : arr) {
                   if (value == 0) {
                       zeroCount++;
                   } else {
                       otherProduct *= value;
                   }
               }

               if (zeroCount == 1 && otherProduct == 0) {
                   return true;
               }
           }
       }

       return false;
   }

   Note:
   - This version uses long, so it can still overflow for sufficiently large inputs.
   - For production or unrestricted integer inputs, use BigInteger or an overflow-safe approach.
   - The exact interpretation should be confirmed: the product of all remaining elements must equal one element in the array.

