201. Code review: What are the problems in the InMemoryCache implementation?

   1. Thread-safety issue: Only get() is synchronized. put(), delete() and size() are not synchronized, so multiple threads can access the HashMaps concurrently.
   2. Inconsistent operations: Two HashMaps are maintained separately. Their updates can become inconsistent when concurrent operations happen.
   3. Memory leak: Expired entries are removed only when get() is called. Unaccessed expired entries remain in memory, and size() includes them.
   4. TTL handling: Use System.nanoTime() for elapsed-time calculations, validate TTL and consider automatic cleanup.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

202. How would you improve the InMemoryCache?

   1. Use ConcurrentHashMap instead of HashMap.
   2. Store the value and expiry time together in a single cache entry instead of maintaining two separate maps.
   3. Use atomic operations such as compute() or remove(key, expectedValue) to handle concurrent updates safely.
   4. Add automatic expiry cleanup or use a cache library such as Caffeine for production use.

   Improved implementation:

   import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
   import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

   public class InMemoryCache<K, V> {

       private static class Entry<V> {
           V value;
           long expiryTime;

           Entry(V value, long expiryTime) {
               this.value = value;
               this.expiryTime = expiryTime;
           }
       }

       private final ConcurrentHashMap<K, Entry<V>> store =
               new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

       private final long ttlNanos;

       public InMemoryCache(long ttlMillis) {
           if (ttlMillis <= 0) {
               throw new IllegalArgumentException("TTL must be positive");
           }

           this.ttlNanos = TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS.toNanos(ttlMillis);
       }

       public void put(K key, V value) {
           if (key == null || value == null) {
               throw new IllegalArgumentException("Null not allowed");
           }

           store.put(key,
               new Entry<>(value, System.nanoTime() + ttlNanos));
       }

       public V get(K key) {
           Entry<V> entry = store.get(key);

           if (entry == null) {
               return null;
           }

           if (System.nanoTime() >= entry.expiryTime) {
               store.remove(key, entry);
               return null;
           }

           return entry.value;
       }

       public void delete(K key) {
           store.remove(key);
       }

       public int size() {
           return store.size();
       }
   }

   Note:
   - This implementation is thread-safe for individual map operations.
   - Expired entries are removed when accessed, but there is no background cleanup.
   - For production caching, I would prefer Caffeine because it provides automatic expiry, eviction and statistics.

